{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779220105,"nonce":"YCdvsMAU2rvYPdy1CaJagE91VGk=","message":"## Spec: task as scenario/environment boundary, not a graph component\n\nCurrent decision: Studio should stop presenting tasks as ordinary model-graph components. A task can still be implemented internally as an adapter when needed, but the product model should treat it as the scenario/environment boundary that generates trial inputs, targets, initial conditions, interventions, eval variants, and timeline semantics for the model.\n\n### Why this should change\n\nThe existing task node is a convenience, but it conflates different semantics:\n\n- `TaskComponent` is not a normal transform in the model graph. It wraps an `AbstractTask` and per-trial `TaskTrialSpec`, and its outputs are generated from task/environment semantics rather than from upstream graph state.\n- The graph UI currently renders task outputs such as `inputs`, `targets`, `inits`, and `intervene` as node ports. Only some of these are honestly bindable model inputs. `inits` are reset/initialization semantics; targets and intervention schedules may feed objectives, metrics, or stage protocol state rather than ordinary component inputs.\n- The graph store builds full-state flow edges from every pair of wired nodes. A task-to-network wire therefore implies the same thick state-flow relationship as component-to-component wiring, which is not the right mental model.\n- The current Scenario Projection Workspace already has `scenario.task_spec` as a first-class draft object. Keeping a duplicate graph task node means the UI has two places that can appear to own task configuration.\n\nRelevant current code handles:\n\n- `web/src/stores/graphStore.ts` creates default `task` nodes and derives state-flow edges from all graph wires.\n- `web/src/data/components.ts` still registers `SimpleReaches`, `DelayedReaches`, and `Stabilization` under `Tasks` with graph-like ports.\n- `web/src/features/scenario/entities.ts` detects task nodes and binds `task_object` entities back to those graph nodes.\n- `web/src/data/rlrmp-model-graph.ts` seeds a `DelayedReaches` graph node.\n- `feedbax/web/serialization.py` still lowers graph task nodes into `TaskComponent` objects.\n\n### Proposed product model\n\nUse one canonical task/scenario object owned by the active `StudioScenarioSpec`:\n\n```text\nStudioScenarioSpec\n  task_spec\n  task_binding_spec\n  value/function/timeline specs\n  eval/protocol overrides\n\nModel GraphSpec\n  nodes = model/mechanics/controller/components only\n  wires = component-to-component model wiring only\n```\n\n`task_binding_spec` should describe explicit bindings from task/scenario outputs to graph inputs, without pretending the task is a graph node. A first shape could be:\n\n```ts\ninterface StudioTaskBindingSpec {\n  schema_version: 'feedbax.studio.task_bindings.v1';\n  exposed_outputs: StudioTaskOutputSpec[];\n  bindings: StudioTaskBinding[];\n  metadata: Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e;\n}\n\ninterface StudioTaskOutputSpec {\n  id: string;\n  label: string;\n  kind: 'signal' | 'target' | 'initial_state' | 'intervention' | 'trial_param' | 'protocol_value';\n  path: string;\n  bindable: boolean;\n  expected_shape?: unknown[] | null;\n  dtype?: string | null;\n  units?: string | null;\n  frame?: string | null;\n  value_spec?: unknown;\n  metadata: Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e;\n}\n\ninterface StudioTaskBinding {\n  id: string;\n  source_output_id: string;\n  target_node_id: string;\n  target_port: string;\n  role: 'model_input' | 'feedback_context' | 'objective_target' | 'intervention_param' | string;\n  metadata: Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e;\n}\n```\n\nOnly `bindable: true` outputs should appear as canvas-edge ports. Non-bindable task fields stay in the task editor as configuration, initialization, validation, or provenance.\n\n### UI direction\n\nReplace the current task tab / task component card model with a persistent task/scenario panel between the component palette and the graph canvas:\n\n```text\n[component palette] [task/scenario panel] [model canvas] [inspector]\n```\n\nThe task panel owns:\n\n- task type selection and scenario variant/inheritance state;\n- delayed-task timeline editor: epochs, signal lanes, length specs, previews;\n- task outputs exposed to the model: cue/input lanes, target signals, perturbation profiles, SISU or other project-defined inputs;\n- non-graph configuration: endpoint generation, catch-trial probability, workspace bounds, sampling scope, eval variants, seeds;\n- explicit controls for promoting a task value to a canvas binding when that is semantically honest.\n\nThe canvas should render a lightweight task-boundary/port strip on its left edge, not a draggable task component node. In React Flow terms, the simplest implementation is probably a non-component node type such as `scenario_boundary` or `task_boundary` whose handles are generated from `task_binding_spec.exposed_outputs`. It should be excluded from `GraphSpec.nodes`, graph component palettes, state-flow edges, component deletion semantics, and backend model-component serialization.\n\nTask-binding edges should have their own visual/data kind, e.g. `task-binding`, and should not create a thick `state-flow` edge. They mean: this model input is bound to this task/scenario output.\n\nThe right inspector should no longer be the canonical task editor. Selecting the task boundary or a task-binding edge can focus the task panel and optionally show a compact binding inspector, but task configuration lives in the task/scenario panel.\n\n### Compatibility stance\n\nGiven the current repo instructions and pre-alpha state, do not preserve saved graph-format compatibility for task nodes. The cleaner switch is:\n\n1. Update built-in templates/examples so tasks live in `StudioScenarioSpec.task_spec` plus `task_binding_spec`.\n2. Remove task components from the draggable component palette.\n3. Raise a clear validation/load error if a new Studio project graph still contains `SimpleReaches`, `DelayedReaches`, or `Stabilization` as graph nodes.\n\nDo not add a long-lived frontend compatibility adapter that silently migrates old saved graphs. That would keep two ownership models alive.\n\nOne caveat: keeping `TaskComponent` as a backend-internal adapter is different from product compatibility. It is acceptable for backend lowering to synthesize a temporary `TaskComponent` or equivalent runtime object from `task_spec` and `task_binding_spec` until the execution path is fully split. The user-facing persisted model should still be task-as-scenario-boundary.\n\n### Backend/lowering impact\n\nThis is not just a visual switch, but it is also not a broad scientific-backend rewrite.\n\nLikely affected areas:\n\n- Frontend graph store: stop including task nodes in default graphs, examples, node rendering, `buildStateEdges`, selection, deletion, and edge serialization.\n- Scenario workspace store/types: add `task_binding_spec` and make task config canonical under the scenario.\n- Scenario entity registry/selectors: represent task outputs as task/scenario entities, not graph ports on a task node.\n- Canvas: add a non-component task-boundary node/edge type or an equivalent React Flow overlay with handles.\n- Component catalog: remove or quarantine `Tasks` from the component palette.\n- Training/build payloads: stop reading task parameters from graph nodes; use `scenario.task_spec` and binding spec.\n- Backend serialization/lowering: accept model-only `GraphSpec` plus `task_spec`/bindings, then construct the runtime model/task/trial wiring explicitly.\n- Tests/fixtures: update default graph, RLRMP seeded graph, provider/serialization tests, scenario entity tests, and browser checks.\n\nExisting `AbstractTask`, `TaskProtocol`, trial sampling, validation trials, and loss/eval methods can stay. The change is mostly the Studio/spec/lowering boundary around them.\n\n### Implementation order\n\n1. Define the canonical frontend types: `StudioTaskBindingSpec`, `StudioTaskOutputSpec`, and `StudioTaskBinding` on the scenario spec.\n2. Update default and RLRMP fixtures to remove task nodes from `GraphSpec.nodes`; seed equivalent `task_spec` and task bindings.\n3. Render a first task/scenario sidebar with task summary/config and a minimal exposed-output list.\n4. Add a non-component task-boundary React Flow node/edge type and store task-binding edges separately from graph `wires`.\n5. Remove `Tasks` from the component palette or turn the existing task tab into the canonical task/scenario panel.\n6. Update scenario entity registry so task outputs, initial states, target signals, and intervention/protocol values are explicit entities/selectors.\n7. Update training payload/lowering to use `task_spec` and task bindings. Keep backend `TaskComponent` only as an internal lowering helper if it is the shortest route.\n8. Add validation that flags task nodes in model graphs as invalid for new Studio projects.\n9. Add focused tests for: no thick state edge from task bindings, bindings serialize outside `GraphSpec.wires`, task config persists in workspace state, and backend lowering receives model-only graph plus task spec.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- Studio graph canvas no longer shows a draggable task component node in new/default/RLRMP projects.\n- Task configuration is edited in the task/scenario panel, not as graph-node params in the right inspector.\n- Task outputs can be bound to graph inputs through left-edge canvas ports.\n- Task-binding edges do not imply full-state flow and do not appear as component-to-component state merges.\n- `GraphSpec.nodes` contains only model/mechanics/controller/component nodes.\n- Execution/lowering still receives enough information to build the task, sample trials, apply initial conditions/interventions, and wire task-provided inputs into the model.\n- Loading a new-format project is unambiguous; old task-node graphs fail loudly or are manually rewritten, rather than silently supporting both models.\n\n### Relationship to next work\n\nThis should inform `bd5aefb` before the value/timeline editor is implemented. The delayed-task timeline and expression-valued parameter work should live inside the task/scenario panel and populate `task_spec`/`task_binding_spec`, not add richer controls to a fake graph task node.\n\nRelated issues: `bd5aefb`, `d64a9aa`, `c540499`, `3fc3e8e`, `0258fac`, `b3ad156`, `c6c6da0`, `e57ae1f`, `93f79f2`.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}